  <header class="page-title">
    <h1>About</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="prose article-body">
    <p>I’m Ajay. I live in Bangalore, India, and come from Sagar, a city in Madhya Pradesh. I’m biased, but it’s beautiful. I have written software for a living since 2020 and for the fun of it since well before that. I’ve always been fascinated by how systems, organisations and people fit together. Software lets me follow those flows, make something new from them and work through the hard parts with other people. Away from the keyboard, I explore cultures through food, play chess badly and keep finding new parts of physics I wish I understood.</p>

    <h2>Now <span class="mono">August 2026</span></h2>
    <p>I’m at Regie.ai, on the systems that move data between customers’ CRMs and their outreach. Most of my day is design and review. The first draft of the code is usually my AI pair’s; the plan it follows is mine.</p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p><em>Design first.</em> I draw the system before I write it: where the boundaries sit, and what can fail. I put that drawing in front of someone before code exists. The diagram on this site started that way.</p>
    <p><em>Test first.</em> I write the test before the code it describes. Fourteen months at <a href="/work/#incubyte">Incubyte</a> made that a reflex rather than a preference.</p>
    <p><em>With an AI pair.</em> Context, then an explicit plan, then review of the plan, then code, then review again, then commit. I spend my attention on the plan; the diff is where the plan shows. I wrote the loop up in <a href="/thoughts/review-the-plan-not-the-diff/">Review the plan, not the diff</a>.</p>

    <h2>On the side</h2>
    <p>Lately I’ve been experimenting with Omarchy and running local models such as Muse Glimmer on my own machine. I’m also reading about Ryan Dahl’s <a href="https://github.com/denoland/celld">celld</a> and learning Rust, slowly.</p>
  </div>
